A||||2||88formula||Need your advice on 88 front polyurethane sway bar bushings, help.||||5||
Z||000000||88formula||06-03-2001||04:01 PM||||I have to replace the old worn out sway bar rubber bushings on my front anti-sway bar from my 88 formula. There is no direct bolt on Poly bushings for the 88 cars so I have to improvise and adapt one from another car. I have the Energy Suspension polyurethane 9.5129G 25MM bushings right now but I have to drill them larger because my bar is bigger, which leaves almost no meat left on them. I would like to know what the guys and girls here have done with there 88 cars?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Img_0367.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||c m west||06-03-2001||04:34 PM||||for my customers who have chosen to go the route of poly on thier 88 swaybars,I have had to put the bushings in a mill here at the shop and bore them out to an I.D. that is .005" under than the O.D. of the sway bar, so to answer your question, just go to a local machine shop and have them drilled out<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||06-03-2001||04:57 PM||||I did mine a few weeks ago.  I used the ones the Fiero Store sells.  Worked fine.  Only problem was getting the old assembly out.  Mine was rusted, and broke.  I had to get a nut splitter and it took me... well, lets just say, it wasn't fun.||||reg||1
